WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.565 --> 00:00:02.940
JIM THOMPSON: Hey, guys, maybe
a little history of Avere.

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.999
We've been around
for seven years.

00:00:04.999 --> 00:00:06.540
I've been with the
company six years.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.570
And we've actually developed
a caching technology

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:13.700
to accelerate
network-attached storage.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.550
If you've seen any movies in
the last four or five years,

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:18.672
and some of the
customers are here,

00:00:18.672 --> 00:00:19.880
we've had something to do it.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:23.370
We've been accelerated
the NAS access

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.300
from the render nodes,
or transcode engines,

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.410
or even the artists.

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:32.960
So We've had a bare metal
machine for a long time.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.140
And just recently, over
the last eight months,

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.550
we've developed a
virtual machine.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:41.570
And we're pretty excited to
have Google part of that now.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:44.300
And over the last
year as well, we've

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:49.160
developed a connector into any
type of object storage device.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:51.720
So we're using S3 API to
connect in to any type

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.730
of either Cleversafe,
Apple data,

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.620
or any software-defined storage.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.330
So we're pretty excited to
have Google part of that today.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.320
So the two offers
we have a Google--

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.250
and again, we're not new
to virtual machines at all.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:18.080
We've actually been
using virtual machines

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:19.840
for a number of years.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.250
Our QA engineering team use
our own virtual machines,

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:28.670
running VMware as a matter of
fact, to do any engineering QA.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:32.180
It keeps the cost down
for billing out hardware

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.204
to do that.

00:01:34.204 --> 00:01:35.620
This Google
offering's pretty cool

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.090
because the storage
that you can use

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:40.220
is just infinitely expandable.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:45.280
And the compute engines that
you would use for rendering,

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.820
you can expand that out
to whatever you want.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:53.790
So what we do is we
offer up a file system--

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:56.840
We're a proxy to a file system,
either from an object storage

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:59.660
device or some other type
of NAS, type of device.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:02.347
So we offer up [? NFS assists ?]
to the clients,

00:02:02.347 --> 00:02:04.430
and it could be artists,
it can be rendered nodes,

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.130
it could be transcode engines.

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:09.479
And then, in turn, we
talk NFS and the S3 API

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:12.460
back to the storage device.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:15.500
So the target industries
are media and entertainment,

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.672
life sciences, and
financial services.

00:02:18.672 --> 00:02:20.630
Like I said, we've been
around for a long time.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:23.250
We have hundreds of
customers out there.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.510
The early adopters were
media and entertainment,

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:28.051
so I think that's why this is
going to be pretty interesting,

00:02:28.051 --> 00:02:29.544
this offering from Google.

00:02:29.544 --> 00:02:30.960
So what I'm going
to do, I'm going

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.766
to talk about
configuration here.

00:02:33.766 --> 00:02:36.140
And if there's any questions,
if I don't cover something,

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:39.570
please ask me later on.

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:42.930
Initially, like I said, we
had a bare metal appliance.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.780
The bare metal appliance was
installed On-Prem, of course.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:47.930
And the reason for
that is to offload

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:52.090
that NAS product, whatever type
of those legacy NAS vendors

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:53.440
you have.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.590
From the IOPS that these render
nodes were beating the living

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:58.510
you know what out
of the storage.

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:00.462
So we're offloading
all that stuff.

00:03:00.462 --> 00:03:02.670
What I mean is that we're
putting the active data set

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:03.950
into our tier.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:05.090
We're a caching appliance.

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:07.200
We're a proxy of
the file system.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.500
We have multiple different
mediums inside of our devices.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.260
We have DRAM for fast access.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.590
We have NVRAM for writes.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:18.060
We have SSDs and SAS drives.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.310
So we move data back and
forth between these different

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.018
mediums, depending on
the active data set

00:03:22.018 --> 00:03:24.890
and what the needs are
from the render nodes

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:27.090
and whatever other clients.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:28.730
So on the bottom
half of this slide,

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:30.420
you have the
On-Prem storage, you

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:33.400
have your render farm,
as well as your artists.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.440
And on the right of that, you
have the NAS and object store

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:37.185
device.

00:03:37.185 --> 00:03:39.560
Not necessarily say that you
would have an object storage

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:41.101
device, but that's
something we think

00:03:41.101 --> 00:03:47.090
will be pretty impressive going
forward because the NAS vendors

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:50.750
cost is getting over the top.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:52.760
On the top, we have the
Google Compute Engine.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:57.310
So we talked about burst
computing a little earlier on,

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:03.320
and if you don't have enough
hardware On-Prem in order

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:05.085
to finish the jobs
that you have,

00:04:05.085 --> 00:04:06.960
it'd be great to be able
to spin something up

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.220
into the Google compute.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:15.570
In addition to that, you can
use Google Storage as well.

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:20.220
But the thing is that in order
to do something-- in order

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:24.190
to really utilize the
compute farm and Google,

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.150
you have to be able to
get the data up there.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:28.120
So what our devices
again-- remember,

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:29.300
I mentioned it's a proxy.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.250
Well, what that means
is if a request comes

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:35.220
in, either the bare metal
machine or the virtual image,

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:37.240
and we don't have it,
it's a cold cache.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:39.750
We're going to go back and
get it from the storage.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.257
So on the bottom, if that
was a bare metal machine,

00:04:42.257 --> 00:04:44.340
if the render nodes make
a request for something--

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:46.230
say it's a new
job, a new render,

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:48.220
a couple shots of
your rendering--

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.100
we're going to go back
to the NAS product,

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.504
grab it, and stick it in there.

00:04:51.504 --> 00:04:53.170
And we're going to
keep it there forever

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:54.910
until there's
competition for space

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:57.330
because obviously the
job's going to finish,

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.470
and something else is
going to be more important.

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:02.030
The same thing happens
for the virtual machine.

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:05.480
On the top left, we
have the Google Compute,

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:09.130
and what we've done is we've
utilized their software

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:11.490
to the build a virtual
machine cluster.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:14.190
We're a clustering solution
of high availability.

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:16.850
So you could build this
cluster out of the 50--

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:19.560
I don't have a customer
that has 50 a cluster.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.710
I think the largest cluster
I've seen is about 21,

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:24.930
but that's not usually normal.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:27.920
But in any event, you
can scale this cluster

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.970
within Google Compute,
depending on how many

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:32.470
IOPS you're trying to drive
with your particular job.

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:39.530
That virtual cluster can also
access the On-Prem storage.

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:42.339
So again, you don't to
copy data up into the cloud

00:05:42.339 --> 00:05:43.130
and leave it there.

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:45.706
You can just fire off your job.

00:05:45.706 --> 00:05:47.580
We're going to go back
to the On-Prem storage

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.580
to grab that data, and
shove it into our cache,

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:50.330
and keep it there.

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:51.870
We're going to
replicate that data

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:55.740
amongst all those virtual
nodes and serve it,

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:59.240
paralyzing it out
to the render farm.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:02.411
So when you're done with that,
when you're done with the job

00:06:02.411 --> 00:06:04.410
and need to output that,
you could write it back

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:07.460
to the Google Cloud storage,
or you could write that

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:10.870
back the way down the On-Prem.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:12.981
We offer our global
namespace that I'm

00:06:12.981 --> 00:06:15.480
going to talk about in the next
slide that kind of goes over

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:19.350
how we can put this all together
in one big globe, so to speak.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:26.920
So since we're a proxy
of the file systems,

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:32.280
we take either the NFS
exports or those buckets,

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:35.270
from either Google Cloud Storage
or your On-Prem object store

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:37.480
device, and we roll
that into a file system.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:39.980
We're going to present that,
even an object storage device--

00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:42.287
we're going to present
that as NFS or SMB.

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:47.230
In addition to
that, what we can do

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:49.890
is we can roll together
a namespace around all

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:53.640
these different NAS products or
object storage devices, as well

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:55.460
the Google Cloud Storage.

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:59.120
And what I mean is that you
could potentially just mount

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:01.970
slash at the top of the
hierarchy and just cd

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:06.564
and change the different
directories or shares that we

00:07:06.564 --> 00:07:08.480
would have representing
these different object

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:13.330
stores, the buckets, or
also the On-Prem storage.

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:16.030
What's really interesting
is that global namespace

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:17.590
is fun and everything,
but I think

00:07:17.590 --> 00:07:19.798
what's really compelling is
if you could do something

00:07:19.798 --> 00:07:22.580
really interesting with it, like
move data behind the scenes.

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:24.510
And the reason
why we can do that

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:26.720
is because we terminate
the protocols,

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:29.720
but we also offer up
own file system IDs.

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:32.220
Again we have to do that for
object storage devices anyways

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:35.450
because there's no such
thing as file system ID.

00:07:35.450 --> 00:07:37.420
Just a common object
storage device, what we do

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:40.090
is, these buckets that
are being presented to us

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:43.180
we gather into our namespace,
we look at those objects

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:45.090
as actually blocks.

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:47.000
So depending on what
object storage device it

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:49.390
is-- it could be a 1
meg, it could be 4 meg,

00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:53.950
actually, block size-- since
we treat object storage

00:07:53.950 --> 00:07:56.540
device a little differently,
we actually cache

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:58.530
the data differently than
we do in NAS product.

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:00.820
We try to keep all the
metadata for all those buckets

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:04.700
that are active and even
keep the first 16 KF files.

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:08.190
We also offer now, that just
came out a couple months ago,

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:11.540
is we offer up a snapshot for
your object storage device.

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:16.080
So if you had either an
On-Prem of your cluster

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:20.620
that you already have, and
you're accessing the Google

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:28.190
Cloud Storage, or you have your
cluster within Google Compute,

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:29.610
we could offer
snapshots for that.

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:32.729
So you could have versioning
for that particular data

00:08:32.729 --> 00:08:35.820
that you're dumping out there.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:38.090
Since we offer up
our FSIDs, that

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:42.230
allows us to do some
gymnastics behind the scenes.

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:44.039
What I mean by that is
it you're able to do

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:45.914
a move or mirror of data.

00:08:45.914 --> 00:08:47.580
And in this example,
what we're doing is

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:51.130
we're actually moving
from your On-Prem storage,

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:54.275
whether it's object storage
or some type of legacy NAS

00:08:54.275 --> 00:08:56.720
product, up into
Google Cloud Storage.

00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:59.500
So we can do that without
anybody really knowing.

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:01.776
In other words, what I
mean is that the clients

00:09:01.776 --> 00:09:03.400
won't know any
different that something

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:07.310
has moved because we're
offering up the FSIDs.

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:09.070
So the FSIDs never change.

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:11.897
So your clients are never going
to see any type of stale NFS

00:09:11.897 --> 00:09:13.105
handle or anything like that.

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:20.270
Here's an example of,
we've seen this slide

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:25.080
a little bit earlier-- This
is an example of actually

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.367
one of our customers,
and there's two,

00:09:27.367 --> 00:09:28.950
there's the one in
media entertainment

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:30.408
and there's another
customer that's

00:09:30.408 --> 00:09:32.190
not in media entertainment.

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:35.100
What they have is
multiple locations

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:37.890
that are accessing
either the Google Cloud

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:41.050
Engine for a rendering, or some
kind of compute of any kind,

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:45.610
or also accessing
the On-Prem storage.

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:48.530
And what's not shown
here is that one

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:51.190
of the things that we do
is we eliminate the latency

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:53.935
that your clients are seeing
when they access that NAS

00:09:53.935 --> 00:09:55.700
storage or that
object storage device.

00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:57.750
So since we eliminate
the latency--

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:01.520
we're a latency killer as
well as an offload engine--

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:04.330
we can place these
bare metal machines

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:06.100
at some other location.

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:09.520
So in this instance, there's
people here in the crowd here

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:12.710
that are using it for
remote WAN caching.

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:15.280
So you could have LA,
Vancouver, New York,

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:18.210
and any other location that
could have these bare metal

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:21.060
machines caching, either
for something that's

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:23.130
stored in the
Google Cloud Storage

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:26.740
or actually caching for your
On-Prem storage that would

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:28.370
be at a different location.

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:31.970
So most of the initial customers
bought the bare metal machine

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:33.810
for just On-Prem acceleration.

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:36.240
And ever since then,
after we came out

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.300
with the feature of
WAN caching, they're

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:40.237
actually putting us
at other locations.

00:10:40.237 --> 00:10:42.820
So what that means is you could
have people Vancouver working,

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:45.210
which is happening due
to the tax incentives,

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:48.250
or like [INAUDIBLE] faxes
in Quebec, and Montreal,

00:10:48.250 --> 00:10:49.226
and everywhere else.

00:10:49.226 --> 00:10:51.350
But the storage is living
at some central location.

00:10:56.600 --> 00:10:58.810
So this is the product line.

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:02.250
We have the Google Virtual FXT.

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:08.610
And that has a certain
size of DRAM and SSD.

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:10.770
And then we have the
hardware off to the right.

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:13.920
And we have three
models right now,

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:16.015
and the farthest one
the right is the 4850.

00:11:16.015 --> 00:11:17.390
It's the highest
performing model

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:18.590
because it's full of SSDs.

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:22.940
The flagship
product is the 3850,

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:29.230
which has a mixture of
SSD, DRAM and SAS drives

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:30.740
for the coldest data.

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:34.510
What we do is, since we have
these multiple different types

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:36.720
of media, we actually
move data, demote it,

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:39.660
promote data back and forth,
within those media types,

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:42.750
depending on what the
client's needs are.

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:46.190
So that in mind, we
also have an NVRAM card

00:11:46.190 --> 00:11:47.510
in the bare metal machines.

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:51.560
So some of the tests we've done
with the virtual image that

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:56.100
runs within a Google compute
is that the speeds are really,

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:59.410
really close to the low-end
bare metal machine that we have.

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:02.060
The only thing that's
different is the writes.

00:12:02.060 --> 00:12:07.360
It's because there's no NVRAM
card in Google Compute yet.

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:10.080
So we have to commit
those writes in the SSDs.

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:14.430
But other than that, the
reads are maybe 80% or 90%

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:19.120
of what the bare
metal machines are.

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:21.250
So just to recap,
the connector that we

00:12:21.250 --> 00:12:24.660
use to talk to the
storage is NFSv3,

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:27.170
or we use the S3
API, which is what

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:30.870
we're using to talk to
the Google Cloud Storage.

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:35.080
We can scale the bare metal
cluster or the virtual cluster

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:36.330
up to 50.

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:38.430
Not that you would do that.

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:43.220
You have to have 150,000 nodes
or something to be able to spin

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:44.660
up your cluster up to 50.

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:49.750
One of the things
that we probably

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:52.250
should have had a slide
on is the statistics.

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:54.600
And somebody mentioned about
statistics a little earlier

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:55.840
on this morning.

00:12:55.840 --> 00:13:00.080
We have about 10,000
statistics that we can present.

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:03.030
It's just dizzying, the
things that you gather.

00:13:03.030 --> 00:13:05.120
And the reason why we can
gather these statistics

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:07.850
is we're in a data
path, of course.

00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:10.060
But we have our head above
water and below water,

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:12.830
so to speak, because we
understand what the clients are

00:13:12.830 --> 00:13:15.930
doing and we also understand
the back-end storage device,

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:19.040
whether it's an object storage
device or a NAS product.

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:22.889
So we can tell you what's
happening with the core filers.

00:13:22.889 --> 00:13:24.680
We can tell you what
the clients are doing.

00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:27.510
And more importantly,
we can drill right down

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:30.010
to tell you what the hot clients
are in any particular point

00:13:30.010 --> 00:13:30.510
in time.

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:34.160
We can tell you the rate of
I/O and what that I/O is.

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:36.900
We can drill down even
deeper and tell you

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:38.280
what the clients are doing.

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:41.200
So we can give you a
list of the hot clients,

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:44.286
and we can tell you what type
of activities they're doing.

00:13:44.286 --> 00:13:45.160
Are they doing reads?

00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:46.076
Are they doing writes?

00:13:46.076 --> 00:13:48.210
Are they doing
directory updates?

00:13:48.210 --> 00:13:52.440
We can tell you the path
of the file to do that.

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:54.660
You can gather that through
our dashboard, our UI,

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:56.830
or you can actually use our API.

00:13:56.830 --> 00:13:59.120
We have an API based
on XML-RPC that you

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:02.170
can use to gather all that data
and put it into any other tools

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:05.990
that you might be using today.

